\begin{theorem}[Classification retaining the given flow]
\label{thm:three-shrinkers}
An admissible three-dimensional static shrinking soliton
$(N,h,f)$ with $\lambda=1/2$ generates a shrinking Ricci flow $G(t)$,
$t<0$, with $G(-1)=h$.
Every supplied shrinking flow associated to these data is, by fixed
identifications at all negative times, a compact round flow, a round
two-sphere times a line, or a quotient of that product by a free
isometric involution.
Every three-dimensional asymptotic limit from
Theorem~\ref{thm:ancient-asymptotic} has bounded curvature on each
slice and such a presentation on its original limit carrier.
\lean{PoincareMT.threeDimensionalAncientAndShrinkingSolitonClassification}
\uses{thm:surface-ancient}
\source{MT2007}
\dcref{Theorem 9.42, pp. 206--208}
\dcref{Proposition 9.46, pp. 209--213}
\dcref{Claim 9.51, p. 215}
\dcref{Corollaries 9.53--9.54, pp. 217--218}
\uses{lem:ancient-heat-contacts,lem:ancient-singleton-level,lem:ancient-soul-coordinates,lem:ancient-terminal-cylinder,lem:ancient-antipodal-collar}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof for admissible static data]
The soliton potential generates the shrinking flow on the original carrier.
The Ricci-nullity maximum principle separates the strictly positive and
splitting cases. In the splitting case a parallel line factor leaves a round
surface; deck transformations give the product or free involution quotient. In
the strictly positive case the potential-level geometry and the surface
rigidity estimates force the compact round alternative. Each construction
preserves the supplied metric and time-dependent identifications.
\end{proof}

